.TH TUI_TTY_IS 3 2026-10-03 "libtuikit 0.1" "libtuikit Manual"
.SH NAME
tui_tty_is, tui_tty_cols, tui_tty_rows, tui_tty_raw_enter, tui_tty_raw_exit,
tui_tty_wait, tui_tty_read_key, tui_tty_read_password \- terminal helpers that need no
session
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <tuikit/tty.h>
.PP
.BI "int  tui_tty_is(int " fd );
.BI "int  tui_tty_cols(int " fd );
.BI "int  tui_tty_rows(int " fd );
.PP
.BI "tui_tty_raw *tui_tty_raw_enter(int " fd ", unsigned " flags );
.BI "void         tui_tty_raw_exit(tui_tty_raw **" state );
.B #define TUI_RAII_RAW __attribute__((cleanup(tui_tty_raw_exit)))
.PP
.BI "int  tui_tty_wait(int " fd ", int " timeout_ms );
.BI "int  tui_tty_read_key(int " fd ", tui_event *" ev );
.BI "int  tui_tty_read_password(int " in_fd ", int " out_fd ", const char *" prompt ,
.BI "                           char *" buf ", size_t " size );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
For a program that is not full-screen all the time: one that sizes a
paged listing, enters raw mode for a single keypress, or asks for a
password.
These are the capabilities of email-cli's terminal layer, with the same
fallbacks, so that email-cli can move onto the library without losing any
(see
.BR libtuikit (7),
"email-cli equivalents").
.PP
.BR tui_tty_is ()
returns 1 when
.I fd
is a terminal, 0 otherwise.
.PP
.BR tui_tty_cols ()
and
.BR tui_tty_rows ()
return the size of the terminal on
.IR fd ,
or 80 columns and 0 rows when it is unknown \(em email-cli's
.BR terminal_cols ()
and
.BR terminal_rows ().
.PP
.BR tui_tty_raw_enter ()
saves the mode of the terminal on
.I fd
and enters the same raw mode as
.BR tui_term_open (3)
(no canonical input, no echo, no signal keys, no flow control), without
any screen sequences or signal handlers.
In
.IR flags ,
.B TUI_TERM_KEEP_ISIG
and
.B TUI_TERM_KEEP_IXON
keep the signal keys and flow control.
Every change is read back; if the terminal did not take it, nothing is
left changed.
.BR tui_tty_raw_exit ()
restores the saved mode, frees the state and sets
.I *state
to NULL; it accepts NULL.
.B TUI_RAII_RAW
(GCC and Clang) restores the mode when the variable leaves its scope, as
email-cli's
.BR RAII_TERM_RAW :
.PP
.nf
    TUI_RAII_RAW tui_tty_raw *raw = tui_tty_raw_enter(0, 0);
.fi
.PP
.BR tui_tty_wait ()
waits at most
.I timeout_ms
milliseconds (negative: no limit) until
.I fd
can be read without blocking \(em input, or the end of it \(em as tg-cli's
.BR terminal_wait_key ().
Unlike that function it tells an interruption from an error: a signal
gives \-1 with
.I errno
.BR EINTR .
.PP
.BR tui_tty_read_key ()
reads one key from
.IR fd ,
blocking until there is one.
It reads one byte at a time, so input after the key stays for whoever
reads next.
A lone ESC is reported after 100 ms of silence, or at once when the input
ends behind it.
Keys are decoded as
.BR tui_decoder_init (3)
describes; the printable character is in
.IR ev\->cp " and " ev\->utf8
(email-cli's
.BR terminal_last_printable ()
and
.BR terminal_last_utf8 ()).
Bracketed paste is not enabled by this function; if the program enabled
it, a paste is reported as
.B TUI_EV_PASTE
without its text \(em use
.BR tui_term_wait (3)
for pastes.
.PP
.BR tui_tty_read_password ()
is email-cli's
.BR terminal_read_password ().
When
.I in_fd
is a terminal it writes
.RI \(dq prompt ": \(dq"
to
.IR out_fd ,
turns echo off, reads one line, restores the mode and writes a newline.
When it is not a terminal it reads one line and writes no prompt.
The line is read with
.BR read (2),
never through stdio; the trailing CR/LF is removed and at most
.IR size "\-1"
bytes are kept, NUL-terminated.
While echo is off, SIGINT, SIGTERM, SIGHUP and SIGQUIT \(em those that
still have their default action \(em restore the terminal before they end
the process: Ctrl-C at the prompt does not leave the shell without echo.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR tui_tty_raw_enter ()
returns the saved state, or NULL with
.I errno
.RB ( ENOTTY ,
.BR ENOMEM ,
.B EIO
when the terminal did not take the mode, or the error of
.BR tcgetattr (3)).
.PP
.BR tui_tty_read_key ()
returns 1 with
.I ev
filled, 0 at the end of the input or when the terminal hung up (then
.I ev
is
.BR TUI_EV_HANGUP ),
and \-1 on error with
.IR errno .
.PP
.BR tui_tty_wait ()
returns 1 when a read will not block, 0 on timeout, \-1 with
.IR errno .
.PP
.BR tui_tty_read_password ()
returns the length of the password, or \-1 at the end of the input or on
error.
.SH NOTES
On Windows these functions behave as email-cli's stub: not a terminal,
80 columns, 0 rows, raw mode unavailable
.RB ( ENOTTY ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tui_term_open (3),
.BR tui_decoder_init (3),
.BR libtuikit (7)
